@@ -128,3 +128,31 @@ Returns `Extension[]` - A list of all loaded extensions.
 > [!NOTE]
 > This API cannot be called before the `ready` event of the `app` module
 > is emitted.
+
+#### `extensions.getUserScriptsAllowed(extensionId)`
+
+* `extensionId` string - ID of an extension loaded in this session.
+
+Returns `boolean` - Whether the extension may use the native `chrome.userScripts`
+API. Defaults to `false` until the application records the user's consent.
+Throws if the extension is not loaded in this session.
+
+#### `extensions.setUserScriptsAllowed(extensionId, allowed)`
+
+* `extensionId` string - ID of an extension loaded in this session.
+* `allowed` boolean - Whether the user has permitted this extension to run user scripts.
+
+Records per-extension consent in this persistent session. Throws if the extension
+is not loaded in this session or does not have the `userScripts` API permission.
+Applications should expose this as an explicit user setting and must not silently
+grant consent when importing or loading extensions. Consent persists across app
+restarts, as do scripts registered through `chrome.userScripts`.
+
+Chromium updates the loaded extension's page and service worker API bindings
+asynchronously. Extensions should check API availability before using it. Revoking
+consent disables future injections; it cannot undo code that already ran in an
+existing document. Reload affected pages after revocation.
+
+This setting grants no additional host permissions and does not enable Node.js,
+disable the sandbox, or relax page Content Security Policy. User scripts run in
+Chromium's extension-associated `USER_SCRIPT` worlds with their configured policy.
